---
// "How it works" in words: the homepage story, one section per caption,
// then who does what, when another tool fits better, and the current limits.
// Every claim follows the usage documents and the ADRs.
import { SupportMenu } from "@/components/support";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/base.astro";

const title = "How Repo Standards works | Repo Standards";
const description =
  "How Repo Standards works: the CLI installs a pinned setup, your coding agent adapts the rest and you review it, and when a template or Copier fits better.";

const REPOSITORY = "https://github.com/lutzseverino/repo-standards";
const REPO_CANON = "https://github.com/lutzseverino/repo-canon";

// each comparison's primary documentation
const DOCS = {
  template:
    "https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/creating-a-repository-from-a-template",
  copier: "https://copier.readthedocs.io/en/stable/",
  copierUpdate: "https://copier.readthedocs.io/en/stable/updating/",
  renovate: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/modules/manager/copier/",
  projen: "https://github.com/projen/projen",
  nxGenerators: "https://nx.dev/docs/features/generate-code",
  nxMigrate: "https://nx.dev/docs/features/automate-updating-dependencies",
  fileSync: "https://github.com/BetaHuhn/repo-file-sync-action",
  healthFiles:
    "https://docs.github.com/en/communities/setting-up-your-project-for-healthy-contributions/creating-a-default-community-health-file",
  skills: "https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills",
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-10 pb-32">
    <nav class="mb-12 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-[15px]">
      <a class="font-bold" href="/">
        Repo Standards
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/">Documentation</a>
      <a href={REPOSITORY}>GitHub</a>
      <SupportMenu />
    </nav>

    <article>
      <h1>How Repo Standards works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Write your setup down once. Your coding agent applies it to every
        repository. This page tells the homepage's short story in words, says
        what the CLI and your coding agent each do, names the tools that fit
        some jobs better, and states the current limits.
      </p>

      <h2>Every repository, set up its own way</h2>
      <p>
        Repositories differ. Each one collects its own agent instructions,
        contribution guide, README, issue templates and checks, written at a
        different time by a different hand. Nothing keeps them in step, so a
        convention settled in one repository is copied by hand into the next, or
        forgotten there.
      </p>

      <h2>Write the setup down once</h2>
      <p>
        A standards source is a published, versioned setup: agent instructions,
        docs, templates and checks, written down once in a public GitHub
        repository and released as versions. A source can offer profiles for
        different kinds of repository. <a href={REPO_CANON}>Repo Canon</a> is a
        ready-made setup to start from, with documentation, contribution, agent
        workflow and GitHub conventions. You can also write and
        <a href="/docs/guides/author-a-source/">publish your own</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Your coding agent applies it</h2>
      <p>
        Adoption applies a pinned version. Ask your coding agent to run one
        command with the source's URL, or run it yourself in a terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>npx @lutzseverino/repo-standards adopt https://github.com/OWNER/STANDARDS</code></pre>
      <p>
        The run selects a version and a profile, records them under
        <code>.repo-standards/</code>, pinning that version and the CLI that
        ran, and applies the version to the repository. Part of the work is
        exact and part needs judgment about your repository;{" "}
        <a href="#who-does-what">who does what</a> splits them. Every change
        stays uncommitted for you to review.{" "}
        <a href="/docs/guides/adopt/">Adopt a standards source</a> walks through
        both paths.
      </p>

      <h2>All alike, and kept that way</h2>
      <p>
        Updates move the pin. When the source publishes a newer release,
        <code>outdated</code> reports it, and running <code>adopt</code> again
        moves the pin and applies the new version, reviewed and committed like
        the first adoption. Repositories on the same pin carry the same setup,
        and each keeps the content it owns. Whether and when to update stays
        your decision, unless you opt in to having every update proposed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who-does-what">Who does what</h2>
      <h3>The CLI</h3>
      <p>
        The CLI resolves the pinned release from GitHub, installs the source's
        exact files and skills, installs the rules they keep satisfying, and
        runs the source's fixes and checks. Exact files replace the tracked
        content at their paths: an edit to an installed file does not survive an
        update. An overwrite that discards edits needs confirmation first, and
        untracked content at such a path, which Git cannot recover, stops the
        run instead. The CLI never commits.
      </p>
      <h3>Your coding agent</h3>
      <p>
        Your coding agent does the contextual work. It reads the source's
        guidance, finds which of your files it covers, and adapts only content
        your project owns, such as its documentation, against the rules the
        source wrote down. It leaves the exact files to the CLI, and the project
        keeps owning what it adapts.
      </p>
      <h3>You</h3>
      <p>
        You choose the source and ask for the adoption. Before committing, you
        review the scope your agent found and every change in the uncommitted
        diff, through your project's usual workflow. With an agent, only an
        unsettled profile choice or an overwrite of a remote setting needs your
        answer during the run.
      </p>
      <h3>Updates</h3>
      <p>
        Updates arrive when you run <code>adopt</code> again, or through the
        opt-in
        <a href="/docs/guides/automated-updates/">update action</a>, a GitHub
        action that proposes every available update as one rolling pull request,
        much like a dependency bot. It never merges: you review and merge its
        pull request like any other.
      </p>

      <h2>When something else fits better</h2>
      <p>
        Other tools share a setup too, and for these jobs they are the better
        choice.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A new repository.</strong> Start it from a
          <a href={DOCS.template}>GitHub template repository</a>, which copies
          its files and directory structure into a new repository with its own
          history, or render it from a <a href={DOCS.copier}>Copier</a>
          template.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Many repositories on one stack.</strong> If their differences
          fit template variables, use Copier: it records your answers in
          <code>.copier-answers.yml</code>, and <code>copier update</code>
          renders the newer tagged template and
          <a href={DOCS.copierUpdate}>reapplies your project's own changes</a>.
          <a href={DOCS.renovate}>Renovate's Copier manager</a> proposes that
          update when the template tags a release. It is deterministic and needs
          no agent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Generated configuration nobody edits by hand.</strong> Use
          <a href={DOCS.projen}>projen</a>. It synthesizes files such as
          <code>package.json</code> and GitHub workflows from a typed definition
          in <code>.projenrc</code>, and marks them read-only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An Nx monorepo.</strong> Use
          <a href={DOCS.nxGenerators}>Nx generators</a> to scaffold, and
          <code>nx migrate</code> to update package versions and run
          <a href={DOCS.nxMigrate}>the migrations</a> that change your
          configuration and code to match.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Identical files in repositories you own.</strong> A file-sync
          action, such as <a href={DOCS.fileSync}>Repo File Sync Action</a>,
          opens pull requests that copy files from one repository to others. For
          community health files such as <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code> and issue
          templates, an organisation's public <code>.github</code> repository
          supplies <a href={DOCS.healthFiles}>default files</a> to every
          repository that has no file of its own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Only agent skills.</strong> Use the
          <a href={DOCS.skills}>skills CLI</a>, <code>npx skills</code>, which
          installs skills for many coding agents and checks them for updates.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Repo Standards fits a shared setup that is mostly prose and process,
        applied across many differently shaped repositories, where part of it is
        exact files and part needs judgment about each repository, and kept
        current by updates you review.
      </p>

      <h2>Current limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          GitHub only: standards sources come from public GitHub repositories,
          and the update action is a GitHub action.
        </li>
        <li>No private standards sources.</li>
        <li>Node.js 24 or 26, on macOS or Linux.</li>
        <li>
          The contextual part needs a coding agent. Without one, the terminal
          walks you through it, and you make the edits yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          One real public source today: <a href={REPO_CANON}>Repo Canon</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  nav a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  nav a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 42px;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .lede {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: 48px;
    font-size: 27px;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.15;
  }
  h3 {
    margin-top: 28px;
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  p,
  ul,
  pre {
    margin-top: 14px;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25em;
    list-style: disc;
  }
  li + li {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  h2,
  h3 {
    scroll-margin-top: 24px;
  }
</style>
